what A Rishi assertion of the form assert x.out contains "key=value" is a SUBSTRING test, so a longer key ending in _key that the same scan emits satisfies it, and the elder wall stops biting while reading green. I met this on my own last lap -- I first named a new reading build_enforced_mute, whose suffix is the elder wall's own enforced_mute=0 -- and booked it as a note rather than an instrument. Measured before claiming rather than reasoned: over 2597 tracked Rishi sources, 5626 assertions bind a contains key to a named scan by let var = run [... path ...] one file deep, 8236 key emissions stand across the fixtures rooms, 347 suffix-ambiguous key pairs sit inside 168 single scans, and the cross reads 93 EXPOSED assertions across 60 witnesses. The heaviest is the fleet's own roster guard: 11 of tools/s/standing_equipment_witness.rish's legs are exposed, among them orphan_plant against deep_orphan_plant and session_orphan_plant, three legs whose whole job is telling those two orphan classes apart. The cure is one operator this tree already owns -- Rishi's bounded matches with a word boundary, matches "\\bkey=value\\b", where _ is a word character so no boundary stands inside mute_asserts. This lap builds one scan reading the exposure tree-wide, a witness walling the swept tools/s/*_witness.rish cohort at zero and reporting the rest, and a control proving in a pen that a planted long key satisfies a contains leg and refuses the matches form. Touches no module source, no ceiling any peer watches, no ledger row and no peer-claimed file.
